WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.770
Thermometer records cover
only the last century or two.

2
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.370
But at Dome C in Antarctica, one ice core,

3
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.450
EPICA, holds a continuous
record back 800,000 years.

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.810
Snow built up there in layers,
and as it turned to ice,

5
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.670
it sealed in small bubbles of air.

6
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.990
So what can these tiny bubbles,
and the ice around them,

7
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.150
tell us about past climate?

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.090
In this mission, you'll learn to read ice
cores and ocean-floor deposits as natural

9
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.750
records of climate.

10
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.170
Then you'll work through a timeline
of glacials and interglacials,

11
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.730
and see how the last
glaciation came to an end.

12
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.510
Your mission: find out what ancient ice
and other natural records reveal about

13
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:43.730
past climate.
